**7. Expected Results & Troubleshooting**

**7.1 What You Should See**

-   C0 baseline: 5--8 of 8 components above 3σ (0.033). All correlations
    positive. This confirms the Hamiltonian produces confinement.

-   C0b control: Similar to C0 but with slightly reduced signal due to
    extra gate noise. Free components should be 0.05--0.12.

-   C3 degeneracy: Free components (4--7) lower than C0b by 15--40%.
    Forced components (0--3) may appear stable or elevated due to
    artificial CNOT correlations.

**7.2 Common Issues**

**All correlations near zero:** Check that you used a 64+ qubit backend.
The circuit requires qubits 0--63. A smaller backend will produce
compilation errors or noise.

**Inconsistent results across backends:** Normal. Individual backend
calibration varies significantly. Pool shots across multiple runs for
stable statistics. Our run-level standard deviation was \~0.025.

**C3 appears stronger than C0b on one backend:** Can happen due to
calibration. The effect is statistical --- it appears when pooling
across backends and shots. Single-backend comparisons may not be
decisive.

**Negative correlations:** Occasional negative values on individual
components are normal noise fluctuations. The average across components
and backends should be positive for the baseline.

**7.3 Validation Checklist**

-   Baseline (C0 or C0b) shows at least 4/8 components above 3σ →
    Hamiltonian is working

-   C0b free \> C3 free when pooling shots → Degeneracy effect detected

-   C3 shows forced-up/free-down asymmetry relative to C0b → Signature
    of propagated degeneracy

-   Bootstrap P(C0b_free \> C3_free) \> 95% → Statistically significant
    support for conjecture

**8. Circuit Difference Reference**

For transparency, here is the EXACT and ONLY difference between the
three circuits. Everything else --- initialization, Hamiltonian layers,
measurement --- is byte-for-byte identical.

**8.1 Circuit 0 (Baseline)**

Step 1.5 does not exist. After initialization (Step 1), the Hamiltonian
(Step 2) begins immediately.

**8.2 Circuit 0b (Depth-Matched Control)**

Step 1.5 inserts self-canceling CNOT pairs at all 8 sites:

> // Site (0,0): identity on comp 0↔1, identity on comp 2↔3
>
> cx q\[0\], q\[1\]; cx q\[0\], q\[1\]; // cx;cx = identity
>
> cx q\[2\], q\[3\]; cx q\[2\], q\[3\]; // cx;cx = identity
>
> // \... repeated for all 8 sites (s=0,8,16,24,32,40,48,56)

Total: 32 extra CX gates. Same qubits touched as C3. Zero degeneracy
created.

**8.3 Circuit 3 (Degeneracy)**

Step 1.5 inserts single state-copying CNOTs at all 8 sites:

> // Site (0,0): force comp 0 = comp 1, force comp 2 = comp 3
>
> cx q\[0\], q\[1\]; // copies state of q\[0\] onto q\[1\]
>
> cx q\[2\], q\[3\]; // copies state of q\[2\] onto q\[3\]
>
> // \... repeated for all 8 sites (s=0,8,16,24,32,40,48,56)

Total: 16 extra CX gates. Creates eigenvalue multiplicity (double zeros)
across entire lattice.

